  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (1633.33) and comparing how often each lottery exceeds this level. This involves evaluating 2500 > 1633.33 (33%) and 2400 > 1633.33 (34%) and choosing B due to the higher probability of exceeding the aspiration. However, the individual does not mention any such reference point or averaging of outcomes. Instead, they compare the probabilities as 'fast die selbe' (almost the same) and choose based on the higher outcome. This suggests a different rule: when probabilities are similar, pick the higher payoff. There is no evidence that the individual computed an aspiration level or evaluated outcomes relative to it. The information considered (outcome magnitude and probability similarity) does not match the information required by the reason (aspiration level and frequency of exceeding it). The justification (choosing higher gain due to similar probabilities) also differs from the reason’s rule (maximize chance of exceeding aspiration). Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this decision reason.",
