  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (1750) and then comparing the probabilities of exceeding this level. Lottery A has a 25% chance (3000 > 1750), B has 20% (4000 > 1750), so the reason would favor A. However, the individual chooses B and justifies this by emphasizing the higher maximum payout and downplaying the 5% probability difference. There is no mention of averaging outcomes, comparing each outcome to a central reference, or evaluating which lottery more frequently exceeds a computed aspiration level. The individual’s reasoning is more aligned with a 'maximax' or 'satisficing on payoff' heuristic rather than the aspiration-level rule based on mean outcomes. The information considered (payout magnitudes and probability proximity) and the justification (favoring higher payout due to small probability difference) do not align with the structure of the decision reason. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this specific aspiration-level rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
